Though its not like we are going to notify them all over the same thread, each is running in its own goroutine, let's take this example to explain this better:

```
func (o .....) Stream(dataChan chan []byte) error {
        var offset int64
        offset = 0
        for offset < int64(len(o.buf)) || !o.closed {
                // buffer is not closed, but we have already read everything.
                // wait for new data.
                if offset == int64(len(o.buf)) {
                        continue
                }

                bytesReady := int64(len(o.buf)) - offset
                dataChunk := make([]byte, bytesReady)
                n, err := o.ReadAt(dataChunk, offset) // ReadAt will hold a RLock
                if err != nil {
                        return err
                }

                dataChan <- dataChunk
                offset += int64(n)
        }
        return nil
}
```

then what will happen internally in our API server is essentially the following:

```
        go func() {
                outputChan := make(chan []byte)
                go func() {
                        for data := range outputChan {
                              response.Send(.....)
                        }
                        close(outputChan)
                }()

                err = o.Stream(outputChan)
                if err != nil {
                        // return error
                }
        }()
```

and a bad client can be represented like:

```
        go func() {
                outputChan := make(chan []byte)
                go func() {
                        for data := range outputChan {
                             time.Sleep(...)
                              response.Send(.....)
                        }
                        close(outputChan)
                }()

                err = o.Stream(outputChan)
                if err != nil {
                        // return error
                }
        }()
```

Now even if second client blocks on line ```dataChan <- dataChunk``` due to slowness reading from the channel, it's only their goroutine that will block, the first client will continue to receive output normally. Please let me know if I'm overseeing something here.
